@kind(api)

@meta{crate: sisho-vault, dependencies: list(), dependents: list(sisho-cli, sisho-counter, sisho-serve)}

=[sisho-vault]

Layer 1: the vault commands that `sisho` does itself.

`check-links` and `convert`. Unlike `sisho-tools`, these use the `tomet`
crates directly, through git dependencies: `sisho` is the part of the
ecosystem that knows what a vault is, as `cargo` knows what a workspace is.

What belongs to the language stays in `tomet`, where its rules live: `new`
(it uses the expression evaluator), `refactor` (the migration rules change
with the language) and `export` (it follows the `@config(export: ...)` a
document declares for itself). `sisho tomet new ...` reaches them.

Each command is a module that does the work and returns what happened as
data. Printing is the program's: this crate writes nothing to the terminal,
so the same work can be shown as text or as JSON. The `tomet` types stay
inside; the program does not depend on `tomet` crates.

==[Module convert]

`sisho convert`: change a document, or a directory of them, from one format
to another.

One verb with two directions. [`Direction::To`] reads Tomet and writes
CommonMark, HTML, Typst or Pandoc's JSON AST; [`Direction::From`] reads
CommonMark or Pandoc's JSON AST and writes Tomet. Whatever the direction,
the input is a file, standard input (`-`), or a directory, and the output
goes to standard output, to `--out`, or next to the source (`--in-place`).

Nothing here is declared by a document. `export` is the one that follows a
document's own `@config(export: ...)`; this is the explicit one, for a
conversion you ask for by name.

The text of a conversion is exactly what is written to a file: no newline
is added when it goes to standard output, so the two never differ.

===[Structs]

====[struct Plan]

Where the result goes, and what else to do about it.

====[struct Item]

What converting one file of a directory came to.

====[struct Tree]

Every file of a directory that was looked at.

===[Enums]

====[enum Target]

A format Tomet can be written as.

====[enum Source]

A format that can be read as Tomet.

====[enum Direction]

Which way a conversion goes.

====[enum Converted]

What converting one file came to.

===[Functions]

====[fn convert_text]

Convert the text `src` of the file `path` (`-` for standard input).

This is the conversion itself, with no destination in it.

====[fn convert_file]

Convert one file, or standard input (`-`), and put the result where `plan`
says.

====[fn convert_tree]

Convert every file under `dir` that is in the format being converted from:
the Markdown files for [`Direction::From`]`(`[`Source::CommonMark`]`)`, the
Tomet documents for [`Direction::To`].

A directory is converted into files, so the plan has to name where they
go. One file that fails does not stop the others; it is an [`Item`] with an
error.

====[fn is_stdin]

Whether a path means standard input rather than a file on disk.

==[Module diagnostic]

How a parse error is shown: the message, where it is, and the line it is
on with a pointer at the column.

===[Functions]

====[fn parse_error]

A parse error of the document `path`, whose text is `src`, shown with the
offending line and a pointer at the column.

==[Module links]

`sisho check-links`: find the links in a vault that point at nothing.

Every `@file`, `@dir`, `@embed` and `@link` that names a local target is
checked against the disk. The check is incremental: what it learned about a
document is kept in an SQLite cache keyed by the document's modification
time, so checking a large vault again does not parse what has not changed.
The cache lives in the user's cache directory, never in the vault.

===[Structs]

====[struct Position]

A line and a column, both counted from 1.

====[struct BrokenLink]

A link whose target does not exist.

====[struct Unreadable]

A document that could not be looked at.

====[struct Report]

What a run found.

===[Functions]

====[fn default_cache_path]

Where the link cache lives unless told otherwise: in the user's cache
directory, one database for every vault on the machine.

====[fn check]

Check the links under `path` (a document, or a directory of them), keeping
what was learned in the cache at `cache_path`.

`Err` means the check itself could not run; a broken link is not an error,
it is in the [`Report`].

==[Module render]

A parsed document, written out in each of the formats `tomet` can produce.

Every function that renders takes a document whose `${...}` expressions
have already been resolved (see [`prepared`]), so none of the converters
needs to know expressions exist.

===[Structs]

====[struct Html]

How HTML is written.

===[Functions]

====[fn prepared]

Parse `src`, the text of the document `path`, in `vault`, and resolve its
`${...}` expressions.

The vault settles what a `${macro.…}` or a `${self.…}` means, so every
command that renders a document agrees about it. A parse error comes back
shown with its line and a pointer at the column.

====[fn meta_title]

The `title` string of the document's `@meta` block, if it has one. An
HTML page prefers it to the file name.

====[fn commonmark]

====[fn typst]

====[fn pandoc_json]

Pandoc's AST as JSON, not a rendering: `pandoc -f json` turns it into
whatever format is actually wanted.

====[fn html]

HTML: a standalone page, or the body alone. A page is titled with the
document's `@meta` title, or `fallback_title` (the file name) without one.
